name: Weekly research for a new error entry

# Runs on a schedule (and can be run manually) to keep growing the reference
# even when nobody happens to file an issue that week. Claude picks ONE real,
# well-documented SAP Fiori/UI5, ABAP (classic or Cloud), Basis, or SAP
# Business One error not yet covered, drafts it, and opens a PR.
#
# Same safety rule as claude-draft-from-issue.yml: never pushes to main,
# never merges itself. A human maintainer reviews every entry before it
# reaches data/issues.json.
#
# NOTE: schedule/cron isn't listed among the "custom automation" examples in
# claude-code-action's docs, but the action only reacts to the `prompt`
# input regardless of what triggered the workflow run, so a schedule trigger
# should behave the same as workflow_dispatch here. [Unverified beyond that
# reasoning - test with a manual "Run workflow" click first before trusting
# the cron.]
#
# Setup required (cannot be done from within the repo):
#   Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> New repository secret
#   named ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: "0 3 * * 1" # 03:00 UTC every Monday
  workflow_dispatch: {}

permissions:
  contents: write
  pull-requests: write

concurrency:
  group: claude-research-weekly
  cancel-in-progress: false

jobs:
  research:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 25
    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v6
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
          token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}

      - name: Set up git identity
        run: |
          git config --global user.email "claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com"
          git config --global user.name "claude[bot]"

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.x"

      - name: Research and draft one new entry
        uses: anthropics/claude-code-action@v1
        with:
          anthropic_api_key: ${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}
          github_token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          claude_args: "--allowedTools 'Read,Grep,Glob,Edit,Write,WebSearch,WebFetch,Bash(git:*),Bash(gh:*),Bash(python3:*)'"
          prompt: |
            REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
            RUN_ID: ${{ github.run_id }}

            You are growing this repo's SAP / ABAP / SAP Business One
            deployment-and-runtime error reference (issues/*.json -> built by
            scripts/build.py into data/issues.json -> rendered by index.html).

            STEP 1 - read first, don't guess:
            - Read CONTRIBUTING.md and scripts/build.py in full. build.py's MODULES,
              SEVERITIES, STATUSES sets and its check() function are the actual rules
              a human reviewer will hold your output to.
            - List issues/*.json and note every title/id already covered, across all
              modules (SAP Fiori / UI5, ABAP / Extensibility, Transport & Release,
              Integration / OData, Basis / Auth, SAP Business One, Power BI,
              Microsoft Fabric).

            STEP 2 - find ONE real, well-documented error not yet covered:
            - Use WebSearch/WebFetch against SAP Notes/KBAs, SAP Help, SAP Community,
              or reputable SAP blogs. Prefer errors that look genuinely common
              (multiple independent sources/threads) over obscure one-off reports.
            - You must be able to cite an actual source URL or SAP Note/KBA number for
              the symptom text and at least one fix. If you cannot find one you can back
              this way within your available turns, do NOT invent one - end your run
              without opening a PR and say so in your final message.
            - If a cause can't be confirmed from your sources, write the rootCause field
              starting with "Not verified. Possibly: " and say what you checked, per
              CONTRIBUTING.md's "don't guess" rule.
            - Never put a real hostname, tenant ID, username, client, or transport number
              in any field - use placeholders like <host>, <TRANSPORT>, <company-db>.

            STEP 3 - write the file:
            - Create exactly one file at issues/<kebab-case-id>.json (id = lowercase,
              hyphenated, describing the symptom, not the app name) with the fields
              title, module, severity, status, verified, msgKeys, symptom, rootCause,
              solutions (array of {label, recommended?, body} - at most ONE item across
              the array may have recommended: true), prevention, refs, createdAt and
              updatedAt (both today's date in ISO 8601).
            - Run `python3 scripts/build.py`. If it reports errors, fix the file and
              re-run until it exits 0 - do not proceed while it fails.

            STEP 4 - open a PR, never merge:
            - Create a new branch named claude/weekly-<run id>-<short-slug>.
            - Commit the new issues/*.json file together with the regenerated
              data/issues.json.
            - Open a pull request with `gh pr create` titled "Add: <error title>",
              whose body names the module and lists the real sources you verified
              against, plus which parts (if any) are marked unverified/possibly.
            - Do not merge the PR yourself and do not push to main directly - a human
              maintainer reviews and merges it, per this repo's review process.
